    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#henry-viii-of-england"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england.jpg" alt="Henry VIII of England" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII of England</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:After_Hans_Holbein_the_Younger_-_Portrait_of_Henry_VIII_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workshop of Hans Holbein the Younger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Since the Anglo-Norman invasion of 1169–1171, English kings had held Ireland as a lordship, nominally granted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#papacy">papacy</a>, but by the early sixteenth century effective English rule was confined to the Pale around Dublin and a few towns, while most of the island was governed by Gaelic and Gaelicised Anglo-Norman lords. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> ended the semi-independent power of the Fitzgerald earls of Kildare after the rebellion of Silken Thomas in 1534–1535. Having broken with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>, Henry no longer wished to hold Ireland by papal grant, and his lord deputy, Sir Anthony St Leger, steered the Crown of Ireland Act through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> in June 1541; the new royal title took effect from 1542. Alongside it St Leger pursued ‘surrender and regrant’, under which Gaelic lords surrendered their lands and received them back as royal grants with English titles: Conn Bacach O’Neill became Earl of Tyrone in 1542, and Murrough O’Brien Earl of Thomond and Ulick Burke Earl of Clanricarde in 1543. The kingdom was a separate realm with its own parliament, courts and administration, but its crown was permanently tied to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, and its creation began a long and violent effort to extend English law, landholding and religion over the whole island.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the sixteenth century much of Ireland still followed Gaelic social patterns, with lordships ruled by chiefs chosen under tanistry, landholding by kin groups and an economy based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>, moved to summer pastures in the practice of booleying. Successive conquests and plantations transformed this world. English and Scottish settlers were planted in Laois and Offaly from 1556, in Munster after 1586 and, most extensively, in Ulster from 1609, and the Cromwellian and Williamite land settlements completed the transfer. Catholic ownership of land fell from roughly three-fifths in 1641 to perhaps one-seventh by the early eighteenth century. The Church of Ireland was the established church, though the great majority of the population remained Catholic, and Ulster Presbyterians formed a large dissenting community. English legislation restricted Irish trade: the Cattle Acts of the 1660s barred Irish livestock from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> and the Woollen Act of 1699 limited exports of woollen cloth, while the linen industry was encouraged and became the mainstay of Ulster. In the eighteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#potato">potato</a> supported rapid population growth, perhaps to around five million by 1800, much of it among a poor rural tenantry paying rent to largely Protestant landlords. Dublin grew into one of the largest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, with Georgian squares and public buildings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#oliver-cromwell"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell.jpg" alt="Oliver Cromwell" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oliver_Cromwell_by_Samuel_Cooper.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Samuel Cooper, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Establishing royal authority across Ireland required a long series of wars. The Desmond rebellions of 1569–1573 and 1579–1583 in Munster ended in devastation and famine. The greatest challenge was the Nine Years’ War of 1593–1603, in which Hugh O’Neill, Earl of Tyrone, and Red Hugh O’Donnell defeated an English army at the Battle of the Yellow Ford in 1598. A Spanish force landed at Kinsale in 1601, but the Irish and Spanish were defeated at the Battle of Kinsale that December, and O’Neill submitted in 1603. The rising of 1641 began a decade of war involving the Catholic Confederation of Kilkenny, royalists, Scottish Covenanters and English parliamentarians, ended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>’s campaign of 1649–1650, with the storming of Drogheda and Wexford, and the completion of the conquest by 1653. In the Williamite War of 1689–1691 the Protestant city of Derry withstood a siege of 105 days, William III defeated James II at the Battle of the Boyne in 1690, and the Battle of Aughrim in 1691 preceded the Treaty of Limerick. Thousands of Jacobite soldiers then left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a>, the Wild Geese. In the eighteenth century Ireland maintained a standing army on its own establishment, and in 1778 the Protestant Volunteers formed to guard against French invasion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#parliament-of-ireland"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland.jpg" alt="Parliament of Ireland" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Ireland_(historical).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sodacan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king was represented in Dublin Castle by a lord deputy, later styled lord lieutenant, assisted by the Irish Privy Council and senior officials. Ireland had its own Parliament of Lords and Commons, but Poynings’ Law of 1494 required its bills to be approved in advance by the English council, and the Declaratory Act of 1720 asserted the Westminster Parliament’s right to legislate for Ireland. English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#common-law">common law</a> was gradually imposed; court judgments early in the seventeenth century rejected Gaelic tanistry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#partible-inheritance">partible inheritance</a>, and new shires extended royal justice into Gaelic areas. During the Interregnum Ireland was absorbed into the Commonwealth and sent members to the Westminster Parliament. After the Williamite victory, the Penal Laws enacted from 1695 barred Catholics from Parliament, public office, the professions, the purchase of land and the bearing of arms, while the Test Act of 1704 also excluded Presbyterians from office. Power thus rested with a narrow Protestant Ascendancy. Its Parliament met from 1731 in the new Parliament House on College Green, designed by Edward Lovett Pearce. Under pressure from the Volunteers and Henry Grattan, the Declaratory Act was repealed and Poynings’ Law amended in 1782, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> legislative independence, though the executive remained appointed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#london">London</a>. Catholics regained the vote, but not seats, in 1793.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Ireland shaped modern Irish history more than any other period. The plantations, above all in Ulster, created a large Protestant population of English and Scottish origin whose descendants form the core of unionism in Northern Ireland, and the land settlements left a division between landlord and tenant, Protestant and Catholic, that drove Irish politics into the twentieth century. Ulster Presbyterians emigrated in large numbers to the American colonies in the eighteenth century, and the Wild Geese served in the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>. Protestant Ireland produced a remarkable intellectual culture: Trinity College Dublin was founded in 1592, and Jonathan Swift, George Berkeley, Oliver Goldsmith and Edmund Burke were among its writers and thinkers. George Frideric Handel’s Messiah had its first performance in Dublin in 1742, and the Wide Streets Commission of 1757 gave the city its Georgian form. The first printed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-testament">New Testament</a> in Irish appeared in 1602. Meanwhile Gaelic poetry and learning survived under pressure, recording the destruction of the old order. The radical United Irishmen, founded in 1791 by Theobald Wolfe Tone and others, introduced a non-sectarian republican ideal of Irish independence that later nationalists claimed as their inspiration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#french-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-revolution.jpg" alt="French Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#french-revolution">French Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Prise_de_la_Bastille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Legislative independence in 1782 proved fragile. Real power stayed with the lord lieutenant and a parliament dominated by the Ascendancy and controlled through patronage, and its refusal to admit Catholics or to reform the electoral system radicalised reformers. Inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, the Society of United Irishmen sought a republic uniting Catholics, Protestants and Dissenters, and Wolfe Tone obtained French support; a French fleet reached Bantry Bay in December 1796 but storms prevented a landing. The rebellion of 1798 broke out in May in Leinster, spread to Ulster and was crushed at Vinegar Hill and elsewhere; a French force under General Jean Humbert landed at Killala in August but surrendered. Estimates of the dead range from about 10,000 to 30,000, many killed in reprisals. The prime minister, William Pitt the Younger, and the chief secretary, Lord Castlereagh, concluded that only union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> could secure Ireland. After an initial defeat in the Irish House of Commons in 1799, the Act of Union was passed by both parliaments in 1800, aided by patronage and peerages. From 1 January 1801 Ireland became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a>, sending members to Westminster, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> ceased to exist; Catholic emancipation, promised informally, was delayed until 1829.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Ireland was the framework within which English monarchs conquered and planted Ireland, transferring land to a Protestant minority and excluding Catholics through the Penal Laws; its plantations, sectarian divisions and the republican rising of 1798 shaped Irish and British politics long after the Act of Union.</em></p>

